<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<section xml:id="sec-rational-short-run-behavior" xmlns:xml="http://www.w3.org/XML/1998/namespace"><title>Short-Run Behavior of Rational Functions</title><introduction><p>Short-run behavior focuses on excluded input values, vertical asymptotes, x-intercepts, and holes.</p></introduction><subsection xml:id="s1"><title>Excluded Input Values</title><p>A rational function is undefined when its denominator equals zero.</p></subsection><subsection xml:id="s2"><title>Vertical Asymptotes</title><p>Excluded input values that remain in the denominator after simplification produce vertical asymptotes.</p></subsection><subsection xml:id="s3"><title>x-Intercepts</title><p>x-intercepts occur where the numerator equals zero and the denominator is nonzero.</p></subsection><subsection xml:id="s4"><title>Holes</title><p>Common factors that cancel create removable discontinuities called holes.</p></subsection><conclusion><p>Rational functions are analyzed through excluded values, asymptotes, intercepts, and holes.</p></conclusion></section>